{"id":"n-20261006-4orpdm","category":"world","display_title":"Supreme Court Weighs Climate Lawsuits","display_title_he":"בית המשפט העליון דן בתביעות נזקי האקלים","short_summary":"The US Supreme Court has heard arguments in a landmark case where ExxonMobil and Suncor Energy seek to block state-level climate damage lawsuits, a decision that could reshape environmental litigation nationwide.","english_level":"C1","words":507,"reading_time":3,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Lascar_The_Outer_Court_-_Forbidden_City_%284497568255%29.jpg/960px-Lascar_The_Outer_Court_-_Forbidden_City_%284497568255%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"Jorge Láscar from Australia · CC BY 2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lascar_The_Outer_Court_-_Forbidden_City_(4497568255).jpg"},"sources":[{"name":"The Guardian","domain":"theguardian.com","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/oct/05/supreme-court-big-oil-climate-damage-lawsuits","title":"US supreme court hears big oil’s bid to block climate damage lawsuits","published_at":"2026-10-05T19:57:36.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-05T19:57:36.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-06T00:12:30.273Z","quality_score":10,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["The United States Supreme Court has commenced its new nine-month term by hearing oral arguments in a pivotal legal battle that could decide the fate of climate change litigation across the nation. In the case of Suncor Energy Inc v County Commissioners of Boulder County, multinational oil giants ExxonMobil and Canada-based Suncor Energy are seeking to dismantle a lawsuit filed against them in Colorado state court. The outcome of this high-stakes confrontation could either pave the way for local governments to hold fossil fuel companies financially accountable for climate disasters or deliver a decisive blow to such efforts nationwide.","The legal battle originated in 2018 when officials in Boulder County, Colorado, sued Exxon and Suncor. The local authorities alleged that the energy corporations had intentionally concealed and misrepresented the environmental hazards of burning fossil fuels. Boulder argues that these companies should bear a portion of the soaring costs incurred by taxpayers to mitigate climate-induced disasters, such as the devastating 2021 Marshall fire, which inflicted over $2 billion in damages. Projections suggest that climate change could cost the state of Colorado up to $37 billion by 2050.","In response, the fossil fuel companies have mounted a defense centered on the doctrine of federal pre-emption. Represented by attorney Kannon Shanmugam, the corporations argue that global climate change is an issue of such vast, international scope that it cannot be resolved through a patchwork of state-level lawsuits. Shanmugam asserted that the Clean Air Act and historical \"federal common law\" dictate that environmental litigation of this nature belongs exclusively in the federal domain, or should be left entirely to lawmakers.","Conversely, Boulder’s attorney, Kevin Russell, urged the justices to focus on the Constitution, questioning what constitutional principle implicitly prohibits state laws from applying to these claims. Legal experts noted that the justices focused their inquiries primarily on the merits of the pre-emption argument rather than questioning whether the Supreme Court had the jurisdiction to hear the case while the state-level proceedings were still ongoing. Dennis Fan, director of the Appellate Litigation Clinic at Columbia Law School, remarked that hearing a case under these circumstances was highly unusual.","With Justice Samuel Alito recusing himself from the proceedings, only five conservative justices remain to decide the case alongside three liberal justices. To secure a victory, Boulder must persuade at least one conservative justice, assuming the liberal bloc votes in its favor. Observers noted that Chief Justice John Roberts and Justice Neil Gorsuch raised skeptical questions regarding the industry's broad constitutional theories. Kirti Datla of Earthjustice observed that while some justices seemed sympathetic to the industry's concerns about the sheer volume of climate lawsuits, they struggled to identify a solid legal basis to rule in favor of Suncor.","Historically, the Supreme Court has resisted similar corporate efforts to block state-level public nuisance lawsuits, most notably during the protracted litigation against tobacco and opioid manufacturers. While the Trump administration has sided with the oil companies, climate advocates and disaster survivors, who rallied outside the court, remain hopeful that the justices will allow local communities to seek justice in state courts."],"facts":[{"fact":"The US Supreme Court began its new nine-month term by hearing arguments in Suncor Energy Inc v County Commissioners of Boulder County, a case where oil companies are trying to block state-level climate damage lawsuits.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Boulder County sued ExxonMobil and Canada-based Suncor Energy in state court in 2018, claiming they concealed and misrepresented the climate damage caused by fossil fuels and should help pay for climate disaster costs.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The 2021 Marshall fire in Boulder County caused over $2 billion in damage, and climate change could cost Colorado up to $37 billion by 2050.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Fossil fuel companies argue that federal law, including the Clean Air Act and federal common law, pre-empts state courts from hearing climate change lawsuits.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Justice Samuel Alito recused himself from the case, leaving five conservative and three liberal justices to decide the outcome.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"The Supreme Court agreed to hear the case while the state-level proceedings are still ongoing, which legal experts 